    body: [
      `It appears in brand decks, agency pitches, and design courses. "Research shows people make a subconscious judgment about a product within 90 seconds, and between 62% and 90% of that assessment is based on color alone." It's usually credited to a 2006 paper by Satyendra Singh in the journal Management Decision.`,
      `The paper is real. Its abstract talks about people judging a product within 90 seconds. The 62 to 90 percent figure is a different matter. It shows up in secondary sources that don't agree on the exact range, and it appears to trace back to older trade writing rather than a measured result. It's a statistic that got repeated until it sounded like a fact.`,
      `## What color research actually supports`,
      `Throwing out the myth doesn't mean color doesn't matter. It means the real findings are more interesting and more useful.`,
      `Lauren Labrecque and George Milne ran four studies, published in 2012, showing that color shifts how people perceive a brand's personality. Red pushed perceptions toward excitement. Blue pushed toward competence. Changing color could even move purchase intent. Lawrence Bottomley and John Doyle found something more important for anyone choosing a palette: what matters most is whether a color is appropriate for the product. Functional products suit functional colors. Sensory and social products suit warmer, more sensory ones. Fit beat any individual hue.`,
      `Henrik Hagtvedt and Adam Brasel showed in 2017 that saturation works like an arousal dial. Highly saturated colors grab attention and feel energetic. Muted colors feel calmer. That's a practical lever: bright for impulse and energy, softened for premium and calm.`,
      `## The button color legend`,
      `The other famous color story is the A/B test where a red button beat a green one by a wide margin. It's real, but it's one test on one page. What made the button win wasn't redness. It was contrast. The winning button was the one that stood out most from everything around it.`,
      `Psychologists have a name for this, the isolation effect, first described by Hedwig von Restorff in 1933: the item that differs from its surroundings gets noticed and remembered. That's why the best design systems treat their accent color like a scarce resource. Apple's site uses essentially one action blue. Stripe uses one indigo. Use your accent only for the thing you want people to do, and it will work whatever hue it is.`,
      `## The honest version of color strategy`,
      `Start with what your category expects, so buyers instantly know what you are. Shift the hue and saturation toward the personality you want. Keep one accent for action. Check every text color against its background for contrast. And extract your real brand colors from your actual files instead of eyeballing them.`,
      `The truth about color is less dramatic than 90 percent. It's also something you can actually use.`,
      `Sources: Singh, Management Decision 44(6), 2006; Labrecque & Milne, Journal of the Academy of Marketing Science (2012); Bottomley & Doyle, Marketing Theory (2006); Hagtvedt & Brasel, Journal of Consumer Research (2017); von Restorff (1933); apple.com and stripe.com CSS reviewed November 2026.`,
    ],
